<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gunpowder">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gunpowder</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gunpowder is an explosive mixture of saltpetre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and sulphur, first developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its use in weapons spread under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>, and later across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It served in the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>, and the military consequences for states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> that faced gunpowder armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunpowder.jpg" alt="Gunpowder" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lord Mountbatten · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Powder-1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
